{:edb/db-before-t 2818 :edb/db-after-t 2819 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536131 50 #inst "2026-10-09T16:03:24.271Z" 13194139536131 true] [17592186128899 1100 "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2-x}+1$?" 13194139536131 false] [17592186128899 1100 "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2-x}+1?$" 13194139536131 true] [17592186128899 1145 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except those that make the denominator zero. To find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x \\in (-\\infty,2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$." 13194139536131 false] [17592186128899 1145 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except those that make the denominator zero. To find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x \\in (-\\infty,2) \\cup (2 ,\\infty).$" 13194139536131 true] [17592186128900 1100 "The domain of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{2x-a}+3$ is $(-\\infty,3) \\cup (3 ,+\\infty).$ What is the value of $a?$" 13194139536131 true] [17592186128900 1100 "The domain of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{2x-a}+3$ is $(-\\infty,3) \\cup (3,+\\infty)$. What is the value of $a$?" 13194139536131 false] [17592186128900 1145 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - a &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= a \\\\\nx &= \\frac{a}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{a}{2}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=3$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{2} &= 3 \\\\\na &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536131 false] [17592186128900 1145 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-a & =0 \\\\ 2x & =a \\\\ x & =\\frac{a}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x = \\dfrac{a}{2}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=3$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\dfrac{a}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{2} & =3 \\\\ a & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536131 true] [17592186128901 1100 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{3}{4x-1}+2$." 13194139536131 false] [17592186128901 1100 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{3}{4x-1}+2.$" 13194139536131 true] [17592186128901 1145 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{a}{bx+c} + d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d$, and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, d)\\cup (d,\\infty)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d = 2$. Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 2$, and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, 2) \\cup (2, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2082/Source/Images/936fa12fa348e9787c6996f12c05b858.png)" 13194139536131 false] [17592186128901 1145 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{a}{bx+c} + d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, d)\\cup (d,\\infty).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d = 2.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 2,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, 2) \\cup (2, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe g